# What CPython prints for all four lines here (python3, 3.12):
#
#   [True, 1, 1]
#   {True}
#   {1: True}
#   {True: 1}
#
# The list row was the shape built tagged first (roadmap Gap R.112, ADR 0259). The set and dict rows
# used to fold their verdicts into a compile-time global that had no tag table, and they refused
# rather than print the number underneath (roadmap Gap R.116). ADR 0384 grew the missing builder:
# each payload is written with the tag that says it is a verdict, so the compiled backend now prints
# the same four lines as CPython.
print([x for x in [True, 1, 1]])
print({True for x in [1]})
print({1: True for x in [1]})
print({True: 1 for x in [1]})
